{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>streams</b> — from the root פלג." "<b>and by streams of water</b> — upon one of the streams, like: 'to the sides [ירכתי] of the ship' (Jonah 1:5), 'and he was buried in the cities [ערי] of Gilead' (2 Kings 13:31), 'the sons [בני] of the asses' (Genesis 32:16). And some say: the sense is that his fruit [פריו] — like 'the sons [בני] of the youths' (2 Samuel 2:21)." "<b>and its leaf does not wither</b> — his wealth all his days, in the manner of good wisdom along with an inheritance." "<b>and all that he does prospers</b> — it returns upon the man likened to a tree. And some say: that 'all that he does prospers' returns upon the tree, for if a branch is taken from it and planted, it prospers. And others said: that it returns upon 'its leaf' [עלהו], like 'and its leaf for healing' [ועלהו לתרופה] (Ezekiel 47:12). And in my opinion: the sense of 'his fruit' [פריו] — the wise soul, that it be filled with the Torah of Elohim, to know its Creator and His deeds that endure forever, and to cleave to its upper world upon separation from its body, like the fruit that ripens on the tree and separates from it, and [the tree] no longer needs it, for it was for the fruit that the tree existed." "<b>and its leaf does not wither</b> — his good memory, in the manner of 'the memory of the righteous is for a blessing.'" "<b>and all that he does prospers</b> — wealth, children, and honor."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {3 "Text reads 'upon separation from its body'; the feminine suffix could refer to the soul's body or possibly 'her body,' i.e., the tree's."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:26:11.457900262Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}